<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Turones</h1><p class="st-meta">300 BCE – 51 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Gallic people of the middle Loire whose Roman town Caesarodunum became Tours.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Turones lived along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>, between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/carnutes">Carnutes</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/andecavi">Andecavi</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bituriges-cubi">Bituriges Cubi</a>. Caesar mentions them among the peoples where he quartered legions and among those who joined the revolt of 52 BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of their society beyond their oppida and coinage.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> their chief town was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Caesarodunum</a>, later called Civitas Turonorum, from which comes Tours. In the 4th century it became the see of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-of-tours">Saint Martin of Tours</a>, and his shrine made it one of the great pilgrimage centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/turones.jpg" alt="Turones" width="320" height="617"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turones_coinage_5th_1st_century_BCE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>300 BCE – 51 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gaulish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>France</dd></dl></aside></div>

